WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.980
Driver, keep your head down.

00:14.980 --> 00:31.820
What you are about to see is a real life story.

00:31.820 --> 00:36.140
Taken from the files of the police racket and bunco squads, business protective associations

00:36.140 --> 00:39.060
and similar sources all over the country.

00:39.060 --> 00:42.140
It is intended to expose the confidence game.

00:42.140 --> 00:46.940
We carefully worked out frauds by which confidence men take more money each year from the American

00:46.940 --> 00:52.420
public than all the bank robbers and thugs with their violence.

00:52.420 --> 01:00.440
Captain Braddock, Captain Braddock, ready.

01:00.440 --> 01:07.460
This piece of rock cost a mining engineer his reputation and almost cost me my life.

01:07.460 --> 01:12.340
It began a short time ago, just two days before I started on my vacation.

01:12.340 --> 01:15.140
Sorry to trouble you, Captain.

01:15.140 --> 01:16.940
There's a character out here that says he's been sold.

01:16.940 --> 01:18.580
Send him to the psycho ward.

01:18.580 --> 01:19.580
Oh, no, sir.

01:19.580 --> 01:20.580
He's not a nut.

01:20.580 --> 01:21.580
Something about a gold mine swindle.

01:21.580 --> 01:26.380
A sucker born every minute and two to take it away from him.

01:26.380 --> 01:27.620
All right, send him in.

01:27.620 --> 01:35.220
Yes, sir.

01:35.220 --> 01:38.500
Evan Davis, you old son of a gun.

01:38.500 --> 01:39.500
How are you, Brad?

01:39.500 --> 01:40.500
Nice to see you.

01:40.500 --> 01:42.500
By golly, you still kept that boy's figure.

01:42.500 --> 01:44.380
That's about all I've kept.

01:44.380 --> 01:48.140
I don't see any fat on that long stomach of yours.

01:48.140 --> 01:50.860
Tell me, how's the mining engineering profession treating you?

01:50.860 --> 01:52.220
Not so good.

01:52.220 --> 01:55.420
I think I'd have done better to become a detective instead of an engineer.

01:55.420 --> 01:59.500
Well, and sometimes I wish I'd followed up the mining engineering.

01:59.500 --> 02:02.500
Sit down, would you?

02:02.500 --> 02:04.940
Tell me, how's Rosemary and Gwen?

02:04.940 --> 02:06.820
Except you wouldn't know Gwen.

02:06.820 --> 02:07.820
She's nine years old.

02:07.820 --> 02:08.820
No.

02:08.820 --> 02:10.300
Hasn't been that long.

02:10.300 --> 02:11.700
It sure has.

02:11.700 --> 02:15.620
Look, Brad, I'm in trouble.

02:15.620 --> 02:16.620
Serious trouble.

02:16.620 --> 02:17.620
Yes?

02:17.620 --> 02:20.980
I've been flim flam, bunkoed.

02:20.980 --> 02:25.300
You're looking at a 24-carat quadruple-plated copper riveted sucker.

02:25.300 --> 02:26.300
You?

02:26.300 --> 02:27.940
Oh, what do you mean?

02:27.940 --> 02:29.580
I've been salted.

02:29.580 --> 02:34.300
I got my company out on a limb for a $25,000 option on a gold mine that hasn't got an ounce

02:34.300 --> 02:36.180
of gold in it.

02:36.180 --> 02:37.980
And the company thinks it's collusion?

02:37.980 --> 02:42.540
Well, I haven't said so in so many words, but I'll never be able to get another job

02:42.540 --> 02:44.540
as a mining engineer unless I can clear myself.

02:44.540 --> 02:47.020
Well, do you know who did it?

02:47.020 --> 02:48.020
Yes.

02:48.020 --> 02:52.820
An old mining engineer with a kind, benevolent face and the most honest baby blue eyes you'd

02:52.820 --> 02:54.300
ever want to see.

02:54.300 --> 02:58.500
He's known affectionately by his fellow townsmen as Dad Miller.

02:58.500 --> 03:00.380
And he got the $25,000?

03:00.380 --> 03:01.380
And my reputation.

03:01.380 --> 03:04.660
Hmm, do you know how he salted it?

03:04.660 --> 03:05.660
That's just it, I don't.

03:05.660 --> 03:11.980
Oh, I know he used gold chloride solution, but I don't know how he managed it.

03:11.980 --> 03:13.940
Did you take your own specimens yourself?

03:13.940 --> 03:14.940
Yes.

03:14.940 --> 03:16.780
And I used my own equipment.

03:16.780 --> 03:21.380
I carried the sacks of samples in a heavy cowhide bank messenger zippered bag.

03:21.380 --> 03:25.700
The old man never had a chance to touch it, put his hands on it, or even get near it.

03:25.700 --> 03:30.260
But somehow he managed to put enough chloride of gold solution on those samples to show

03:30.260 --> 03:33.300
an essay report averaging $400 to the ton.

03:33.300 --> 03:38.100
I know it would only take a few drops of gold chloride in each sample sack to show that.

03:38.100 --> 03:40.380
I know, but he had to get those few drops in there.

03:40.380 --> 03:42.020
Well, he just couldn't have done it.

03:42.020 --> 03:43.020
It's impossible.

03:43.020 --> 03:44.020
Hmm.

03:44.020 --> 03:45.020
Where did this take place?

03:45.020 --> 03:46.020
Up in the mountains.

03:46.020 --> 03:47.020
A few miles above Eureka's Plain.

03:47.020 --> 03:48.020
That's on the Tawooey River, isn't it?

03:48.020 --> 03:49.020
Yeah, it runs right past the town.

03:49.020 --> 03:57.300
That's supposed to be one of the best trough streams in the state.

03:57.300 --> 03:58.300
It is.

03:58.300 --> 04:01.620
I caught some beautiful rainbows while I was up there getting rooked.

04:01.620 --> 04:06.100
And Wolf's Head Lake up above there is stacked with German browns and speckles.

04:06.100 --> 04:09.100
I'm starting on my vacation day after tomorrow.

04:09.100 --> 04:14.500
I was going up to Selden, but do you suppose if I set myself up as a pigeon that this Dad

04:14.500 --> 04:15.900
Miller would make a try for me?

04:15.900 --> 04:16.900
I'm sure of it.

04:16.900 --> 04:21.180
All you have to do is make a noise like ready money and he'll be there with bells on.

04:21.180 --> 04:23.900
Where's the best place to contact him?

04:23.900 --> 04:26.380
At the Blue Diamond Saloon in Eureka Springs.

04:26.380 --> 04:29.060
He drops in there every afternoon for a couple of beers.

04:29.060 --> 04:31.140
I'll have to get a sample outfit from you.

04:31.140 --> 04:32.740
I don't know what's happened to mine.

04:32.740 --> 04:35.220
It's been such a long time since I've done any prospecting.

04:35.220 --> 04:36.780
Don't worry about that.

04:36.780 --> 04:38.820
I'll fix you up with everything you need.

04:38.820 --> 04:42.100
Brad, I can't tell you how grateful I am to you.

04:42.100 --> 04:43.100
Now, wait a minute.

04:43.100 --> 04:45.060
Don't get your hopes up too high.

04:45.060 --> 04:48.700
If this old conjure was tricky enough to fool you, I'm not so sure that I can crack this

04:48.700 --> 04:49.700
case.

04:49.700 --> 04:50.700
But I'm willing to try.

04:50.700 --> 04:54.140
Now, if you'll just fill me in on the main points, I'll know what to be on the lookout

04:54.140 --> 04:55.140
for.

04:55.140 --> 04:58.820
Why do you think these crooks always follow the same pattern once they find a nice angle?

04:58.820 --> 05:01.980
Well, he wrote to the company and said he had a good prospect.

05:01.980 --> 05:05.020
Asked for an engineer to come up to check it.

05:05.020 --> 05:07.260
The company sent me.

05:07.260 --> 05:09.900
I took three sets of samples on three different occasions.

05:09.900 --> 05:14.500
As I told you, I never let those samples get out of my sight for a minute.

05:14.500 --> 05:18.340
I even personally took them over to Rimrock and had them assayed there.

05:18.340 --> 05:22.620
And later on, the company sent some of the other samples up to government assayers in

05:22.620 --> 05:24.220
San Francisco.

05:24.220 --> 05:25.260
Almost the same report.

05:25.260 --> 05:28.380
Right around $400 to the ton.

05:28.380 --> 05:34.220
So on my recommendation, the company paid this old crook $25,000 for an option.

05:34.220 --> 05:39.380
They sent a crew up there with diamond drills to make a preliminary exploration.

05:39.380 --> 05:44.220
The crew sent back over six tons of cores and face specimens, and there wasn't a grain

05:44.220 --> 05:47.300
of gold in the entire six tons.

05:47.300 --> 05:48.940
Every assay came back negative.

05:48.940 --> 05:50.740
They didn't even show color.

05:50.740 --> 05:55.020
And you're sure that he didn't have any opportunity to tamper with your original samples, the

05:55.020 --> 05:56.860
ones that assayed around $400?

05:56.860 --> 05:58.620
I'm positive of it.

05:58.620 --> 05:59.620
That's the point that's...

05:59.620 --> 06:00.620
Well, it's driving me nuts.

06:00.620 --> 06:05.420
If I'd been careless or if there'd been the slightest chance of a slip-up, it'd be different.

06:05.420 --> 06:07.340
But I wasn't careless.

06:07.340 --> 06:11.340
The only thing I can figure is the old crook must be a magician.

06:11.340 --> 06:13.580
You know, that's probably the answer.

06:13.580 --> 06:14.580
What do you mean?

06:14.580 --> 06:17.500
You've seen magicians perform the impossible on the stage, haven't you?

06:17.500 --> 06:19.980
Well, I've seen them do things that looked impossible.

06:19.980 --> 06:24.300
Yes, the magician's art depends upon one little thing, misdirection.

06:24.300 --> 06:26.780
You think you see what's happening, but you don't.

06:26.780 --> 06:28.740
But I never even let him get near the bag.

06:28.740 --> 06:31.860
You can't put gold chloride on samples without getting near them.

06:31.860 --> 06:34.740
A heavy cowhide bag with a padlock on it.

06:34.740 --> 06:38.460
And besides, Brad, you know I know, but there was a slip-up somewhere along the line.

06:38.460 --> 06:39.460
There had to be.

06:39.460 --> 06:42.380
Now, you stick around where I can reach you by the telephone for the next few days.

06:42.380 --> 06:44.580
I may need your help.

06:44.580 --> 06:46.660
What about the Bureau of Mines?

06:46.660 --> 06:49.100
They sent a field engineer up there to investigate.

06:49.100 --> 06:50.540
He ran into a blank wall.

06:50.540 --> 06:51.540
I see.

06:51.540 --> 06:55.940
Well, bring me the tools and things I'll need here at the office sometime tomorrow.

06:55.940 --> 06:57.740
I'll have them here within an hour.

06:57.740 --> 06:59.700
Okay, and keep your chins up.

06:59.700 --> 07:01.700
Raddick's on the trail.

07:01.700 --> 07:21.420
So long.

07:21.420 --> 07:23.820
Things are kind of quiet around here, aren't they?

07:23.820 --> 07:24.820
Quiet?

07:24.820 --> 07:26.620
Judas, my friend, this town just isn't quiet.

07:26.620 --> 07:27.620
It's dead.

07:27.620 --> 07:29.100
Old Ricky Mortis has already set in.

07:29.100 --> 07:33.460
If it wasn't for an occasional trout fisherman like yourself, I'd starve to death, which

07:33.460 --> 07:34.460
I'm going to do anyway.

07:34.460 --> 07:36.460
Cold all played out, huh?

07:36.460 --> 07:37.460
No.

07:37.460 --> 07:40.340
There's more gold than those hills has ever been taken out.

07:40.340 --> 07:41.340
Gold's still there.

07:41.340 --> 07:43.780
Trouble is, nobody knows where to find it.

07:43.780 --> 07:44.780
You a mining man?

07:44.780 --> 07:45.780
No, not exactly.

07:45.780 --> 07:49.780
Although, I did take a couple of years of geology and mining engineering.

07:49.780 --> 07:53.180
No, I'm in the investment business.

07:53.180 --> 07:55.100
Investments in the development of mining property.

07:55.100 --> 07:57.420
Going to be around as long?

07:57.420 --> 07:59.820
Oh, a couple of weeks if the fish keep biting.

07:59.820 --> 08:02.740
Well, I'll be around here to serve you any time you get thirsty.

08:02.740 --> 08:05.540
They ain't got nothing to drink over in that hotel except coffee.

08:05.540 --> 08:07.540
You can call that stuff coffee.

08:07.540 --> 08:08.540
Okay, thanks.

08:08.540 --> 08:09.540
Thank you.

08:09.540 --> 08:10.540
Hey, Jeb, you ain't finished your beer.

08:10.540 --> 08:11.540
You drink it.

08:11.540 --> 08:12.540
What's the matter with it?

08:12.540 --> 08:13.540
You got a bug in it or something?

08:13.540 --> 08:33.940
Come in.

08:33.940 --> 08:34.940
How is it?

08:34.940 --> 08:37.940
I hope I ain't disturbing you, no?

08:37.940 --> 08:40.940
No, not at all.

08:40.940 --> 08:43.940
I happen to see a light come down on the street.

08:43.940 --> 08:46.940
I thought maybe you wasn't in bed, so I thought I'd come up and have a little talk with you.

08:46.940 --> 08:48.940
Oh, won't you come in?

08:48.940 --> 08:49.940
Thank you.

08:49.940 --> 08:52.940
Miller's my name, sir.

08:52.940 --> 08:55.940
Folks in these parts call me Dad.

08:55.940 --> 08:56.940
Dad Miller.

08:56.940 --> 08:58.940
Bradley's my name, Mr. Miller.

08:58.940 --> 09:00.940
Oh, won't you sit down?

09:00.940 --> 09:01.940
Thank you.

09:01.940 --> 09:02.940
Thank you.

09:02.940 --> 09:06.940
Hey, caught any fish yet?

09:06.940 --> 09:08.940
I just got here this afternoon.

09:08.940 --> 09:09.940
I haven't had a chance.

09:09.940 --> 09:11.940
I'm going to take a shot at them in the morning.

09:11.940 --> 09:16.940
I stopped in at the Blue Diamond this evening, and my pal Sam Kelsey tells me that you might

09:16.940 --> 09:18.940
be interested in mining and development.

09:18.940 --> 09:20.940
I'm more interested in trout fishing.

09:20.940 --> 09:22.940
I'm on my vacation.

09:22.940 --> 09:28.940
Well, I ain't a gambling man, but I'll bet I could show you something up in the mountains

09:28.940 --> 09:31.940
that would make you forget all about trout fishing.

09:31.940 --> 09:35.940
That is, if you're interested in gold.

09:35.940 --> 09:37.940
I'm always interested in gold.

09:37.940 --> 09:39.940
But why did you come to me?

09:39.940 --> 09:43.940
Well, the folks around here are a sly lot.

09:43.940 --> 09:48.940
They think I'm an old fool, but the laugh's going to be on them when the truth comes out.

09:48.940 --> 09:50.940
Well, what is the truth?

09:50.940 --> 09:52.940
Well, I don't want you to take my word for it.

09:52.940 --> 09:55.940
All I want you to do is to see for yourself.

09:55.940 --> 10:00.940
I'll take you up there in my Jeep and let you take out some samples from any part of

10:00.940 --> 10:01.940
the vein you want.

10:01.940 --> 10:06.940
Then all you've got to do is sit back and let the assayer give you the answer.

10:06.940 --> 10:07.940
How much does it run?

10:07.940 --> 10:09.940
Runs about 400 to the ton.

10:09.940 --> 10:13.940
And if it don't run that much, I'll eat this here hat without cream or sugar.

10:13.940 --> 10:14.940
Is it free milling?

10:14.940 --> 10:15.940
No, sir.

10:15.940 --> 10:16.940
It's sulfide.

10:16.940 --> 10:19.940
It looks all the world like bull quarts.

10:19.940 --> 10:22.940
Has to be processed.

10:22.940 --> 10:24.940
That's why I'm not operating it myself.

10:24.940 --> 10:29.940
It'll cost about $8,000 to $10,000 to lay in a load up there.

10:29.940 --> 10:35.940
Then we have to put in a compressor and pipe some water up there to hold down the dust.

10:35.940 --> 10:38.940
That'll cost another $15,000 or $20,000.

10:38.940 --> 10:41.940
But when we get it set, we'll have a bonanza.

10:41.940 --> 10:42.940
Why?

10:42.940 --> 10:47.940
Why, I've got over a thousand ton exposed in my little gopher hole right now.

10:47.940 --> 10:48.940
Sounds good.

10:48.940 --> 10:49.940
Almost too good to be true.

10:49.940 --> 10:51.940
Seeing is believing.

10:51.940 --> 10:52.940
That's right.

10:52.940 --> 10:55.940
You got any equipment with you to take out some samples?

10:55.940 --> 10:57.940
I got enough to make out.

10:57.940 --> 11:01.940
I always bring along a little gear when I go fishing in case I want to pick up a specimen.

11:01.940 --> 11:02.940
Good.

11:02.940 --> 11:03.940
Always like a man to use his own equipment.

11:03.940 --> 11:06.940
Then he don't have no suspicions or doubts in his mind.

11:06.940 --> 11:08.940
All right, Mr. Miller, what time?

11:08.940 --> 11:10.940
8 o'clock too early?

11:10.940 --> 11:11.940
That's all right.

11:11.940 --> 11:14.940
Pick you up here at 8 o'clock in the morning with my jeep.

11:14.940 --> 11:15.940
I'll bring the lunch.

11:15.940 --> 11:20.940
All you got to do is sit back and ride in style.

11:20.940 --> 11:23.940
Hey, I hope we can keep this just between ourselves.

11:23.940 --> 11:27.940
I don't want to start a stampede up there until I can stake out some more claims.

11:27.940 --> 11:31.940
If you handle it right, I'll stake out a claim for you too.

11:31.940 --> 11:33.940
Don't you worry about me, Mr. Miller.

11:33.940 --> 11:36.940
You can defend upon me to play it close to my chest.

11:36.940 --> 11:37.940
Good, good.

11:37.940 --> 11:41.940
It's great to do business with an intelligent man.

11:41.940 --> 11:42.940
Good night, sir.

11:42.940 --> 11:44.940
I'll be seeing you in the morning.

11:44.940 --> 11:46.940
Good night, Mr. Miller.

11:54.940 --> 11:56.940
Well, here we are, Mr. Paddock.

11:56.940 --> 11:59.940
Another million dollars overhauled.

11:59.940 --> 12:01.940
You got everything set?

12:01.940 --> 12:02.940
Yeah, I think so.

12:02.940 --> 12:04.940
Well, you better take that.

12:04.940 --> 12:06.940
Come on, let's go get those specimens.

12:09.940 --> 12:10.940
All ready?

12:10.940 --> 12:11.940
Yeah, I think so.

12:11.940 --> 12:13.940
I'll be waiting.

12:23.940 --> 12:26.940
Well, that's the way I like to see a man settle.

12:26.940 --> 12:29.940
You dig deep and find out what's really in there.

12:29.940 --> 12:32.940
It's the only way to make a real preliminary test.

12:32.940 --> 12:34.940
Yes, sir.

12:34.940 --> 12:37.940
You want me to get some mud to get a tamper with?

12:37.940 --> 12:38.940
No, thank you.

12:38.940 --> 12:40.940
I brought some clay along with me.

12:42.940 --> 12:45.940
By Jingo, you certainly know your business, young fellow.

12:47.940 --> 12:49.940
Nobody's ever going to assault you.

12:49.940 --> 12:51.940
I'd like to see them try.

12:51.940 --> 12:53.940
They'd never get away with it.

12:53.940 --> 12:59.940
Not with you using your own tools, your own powder, and your own mud.

13:02.940 --> 13:05.940
There's a neat little badge you got, too, to put your specimens in.

13:06.940 --> 13:08.940
Fabric proof, huh?

13:12.940 --> 13:13.940
Yes, sir.

13:13.940 --> 13:23.940
Yeah, that ought to hold it.

13:26.940 --> 13:28.940
All right, Mr. Miller, after you.

13:28.940 --> 13:30.940
Yes, sir.

13:30.940 --> 13:32.940
Oh, my.

13:41.940 --> 13:43.940
There she blows.

13:43.940 --> 13:48.940
Well, while we're waiting for those powder fumes to blow away, we might as well have our lunch.

13:49.940 --> 13:51.940
Sounds like a good idea to me.

13:51.940 --> 13:53.940
Yes.

13:53.940 --> 13:55.940
Hope you like fried chicken.

13:55.940 --> 13:56.940
I sure do.

13:56.940 --> 14:05.940
What's the matter?

14:05.940 --> 14:07.940
Too salty?

14:07.940 --> 14:09.940
No, it's all right.

14:09.940 --> 14:12.940
That cook over at the elite cafe always did have a heavy hand with salt.

14:12.940 --> 14:15.940
But salt's good for you.

14:15.940 --> 14:19.940
Show me a man who eats plenty of salt, and I'll show you a healthy man.

14:19.940 --> 14:21.940
Let's have some tater salad.

14:21.940 --> 14:22.940
All right.

14:22.940 --> 14:37.940
Here we are.

14:37.940 --> 14:40.940
Boy, that cook sure does have a heavy hand with the salt.

14:40.940 --> 14:44.940
Yeah, it's mighty salty, all right.

14:44.940 --> 14:46.940
How'd you like a nice drink of spring water?

14:46.940 --> 14:51.940
I sure could use it.

14:51.940 --> 14:54.940
Oh, I'll be hog-tied, throat-and-blended.

14:54.940 --> 14:57.940
That no-good cook forgot to fill my canteen.

14:57.940 --> 15:01.940
Just goes to show you if you want anything done right, you gotta do it yourself.

15:01.940 --> 15:02.940
No water?

15:02.940 --> 15:03.940
No.

15:03.940 --> 15:05.940
Well, never mind.

15:05.940 --> 15:09.940
There's the sweetest thing you ever saw about three miles down the road.

15:09.940 --> 15:13.940
And when I get back to town, I'll give that cook a bawling out he'll never forget.

15:13.940 --> 15:16.940
I'll pick up the specimens and be right with you.

15:16.940 --> 15:19.940
And I'll have that jeep all packed up and ready to go when you come out.

15:19.940 --> 15:31.940
Fine.

15:31.940 --> 15:32.940
All set?

15:32.940 --> 15:34.940
All set.

15:34.940 --> 15:40.940
Well, off in the car we go.

15:40.940 --> 15:45.940
Mr. Barrett, this time I won't disappoint you about that cool drink of sparkling spring water.

15:45.940 --> 15:52.940
Come on, spring's down this way about 20 paces.

15:52.940 --> 15:54.940
Here we are, Mr. Barrett.

15:54.940 --> 15:58.940
Here we are.

15:58.940 --> 16:00.940
There we are.

16:00.940 --> 16:08.940
There's nothing like a cool drink of spring water when a man's really thirsty.

16:08.940 --> 16:13.940
Well, sir, you've still got plenty of time to get to Enrock before the assay office is closed.

16:13.940 --> 16:15.940
There are five or six of them there, so you can take your pick.

16:15.940 --> 16:16.940
They're all good.

16:16.940 --> 16:20.940
Now, you're sure this stuff will assay around $400 a ton?

16:20.940 --> 16:21.940
Positive.

16:21.940 --> 16:23.940
Well, this I've got to see.

16:23.940 --> 16:25.940
You'll see, all right.

16:25.940 --> 16:29.940
You'll see.

16:29.940 --> 16:30.940
Hello?

16:30.940 --> 16:31.940
Oh, hello, Brad.

16:31.940 --> 16:32.940
Were they good?

16:32.940 --> 16:34.940
Every one of them.

16:34.940 --> 16:38.940
I paid an extra fee to have them rush through a trial assay, and they all came out the same.

16:38.940 --> 16:40.940
Averaging around $400.

16:40.940 --> 16:43.940
Well, from your description, that's the identical tunnel,

16:43.940 --> 16:46.940
and I know positively there isn't a grain of gold in there.

16:46.940 --> 16:53.940
By the way, when Miller took you up there for those samples, did he offer to furnish the lunch?

16:53.940 --> 16:55.940
Uh-huh, and was it very salty?

16:55.940 --> 17:00.940
I know you don't solve gold mines with salt, but in this case, I'm not so sure.

17:00.940 --> 17:01.940
I've got a hunch.

17:01.940 --> 17:03.940
I'm going up there tomorrow and pick up some more samples.

17:03.940 --> 17:05.940
Now, this is what I want you to do.

17:05.940 --> 17:07.940
You're familiar with the layout up there, so...

17:07.940 --> 17:10.940
I had a feeling that my bag had been tampered with,

17:10.940 --> 17:36.940
so the next time I went up to make another test, I made sure that somebody would be in for a big surprise.

17:36.940 --> 17:39.940
Don't have to worry about salt this time, Mr. Braddock.

17:39.940 --> 17:41.940
I made these sandwiches myself.

17:41.940 --> 17:42.940
Hope you like corned beef.

17:42.940 --> 17:45.940
There's nothing wrong with corned beef.

17:45.940 --> 17:48.940
I'm glad you decided to double-check, my little prospect, Mr. Braddock.

17:48.940 --> 17:55.940
A man can't be too careful in cases like this, when it involves money, real money, I mean.

17:55.940 --> 17:58.940
You didn't put red pepper on this, did you?

17:58.940 --> 18:00.940
Me? No.

18:00.940 --> 18:02.940
No, I don't think so.

18:02.940 --> 18:05.940
It does taste a mite peppery, though.

18:05.940 --> 18:10.940
It's funny, just the way it came out of the can.

18:10.940 --> 18:12.940
Maybe you'd like a drink of water?

18:12.940 --> 18:13.940
Yeah, thanks.

18:13.940 --> 18:16.940
I got this out of my own well.

18:16.940 --> 18:25.940
There you are.

18:25.940 --> 18:26.940
What's the matter?

18:26.940 --> 18:28.940
Don't you like the taste?

18:28.940 --> 18:30.940
It's soapy water.

18:30.940 --> 18:31.940
Takes a little time to get used to it.

18:31.940 --> 18:33.940
Took me about ten years.

18:33.940 --> 18:34.940
I'm mighty fond of it, though, now.

18:34.940 --> 18:37.940
I'm afraid it would take me a lot longer than ten years.

18:37.940 --> 18:43.940
Well, if you don't like the taste, we'll drive down to the spring and get you some fresh water.

18:43.940 --> 18:52.940
Let's go.

18:52.940 --> 18:56.940
What's that?

18:56.940 --> 18:57.940
What's the matter?

18:57.940 --> 19:00.940
It's my eyes. I'm blind. I can't see nothing. It's my eyes.

19:00.940 --> 19:02.940
Your eyes will be all right in a few minutes.

19:02.940 --> 19:04.940
Except tear gas don't last long.

19:04.940 --> 19:05.940
So you're a cop.

19:05.940 --> 19:06.940
That's right.

19:06.940 --> 19:09.940
Stinking, sneaking cop.

19:09.940 --> 19:11.940
Better reach, Mr. Copper, before I blow you to two.

19:11.940 --> 19:14.940
And now I've got to teach you it ain't healthy to go squirting tear gas in my...

19:14.940 --> 19:15.940
None of that yet.

19:15.940 --> 19:16.940
Why?

19:16.940 --> 19:20.940
Because I have other plans for Mr. Braddock.

19:20.940 --> 19:22.940
You get in the Jeep and drive.

19:22.940 --> 19:23.940
Come on.

19:23.940 --> 19:36.940
You take the car. I'll keep him covered.

19:36.940 --> 19:37.940
I don't get it.

19:37.940 --> 19:39.940
What's the sense of fooling around like this?

19:39.940 --> 19:41.940
Why don't we just knock him on the head and throw him over in a gulch somewhere?

19:41.940 --> 19:42.940
Because that's murder.

19:42.940 --> 19:43.940
Shut up.

19:43.940 --> 19:44.940
He's right.

19:44.940 --> 19:46.940
That is murder.

19:46.940 --> 19:48.940
And murder is against the law.

19:48.940 --> 19:51.940
Even more against the law than salt in the mine.

19:51.940 --> 19:56.940
Well, if you think I'm going to do a ten-year stretch just because you're too chicken to put this guy away, you're nuts.

19:56.940 --> 19:59.940
Nobody ain't going to do no ten-year stretch.

19:59.940 --> 20:03.940
Because Mr. Braddock ain't going to tell anyone we salted a mine.

20:03.940 --> 20:04.940
I'm not?

20:04.940 --> 20:06.940
No, sir.

20:06.940 --> 20:09.940
Not unless you do it with a Ouija board.

20:09.940 --> 20:10.940
What's that?

20:10.940 --> 20:15.940
One of them gimmicks that they use to send messages back from the dear departed.

20:15.940 --> 20:17.940
I thought you said we weren't going to knock him off.

20:17.940 --> 20:18.940
We ain't.

20:18.940 --> 20:22.940
But mining is a dangerous occupation.

20:22.940 --> 20:25.940
And mines is dangerous places.

20:25.940 --> 20:28.940
Anything can happen in a mine.

20:28.940 --> 20:30.940
Anything at all.

20:30.940 --> 20:37.940
When a man gets himself killed in a mine, that's an accident.

20:37.940 --> 20:38.940
Not murder.

20:38.940 --> 20:42.940
And accidents is legal.

20:42.940 --> 20:43.940
Very pretty.

20:43.940 --> 20:44.940
But you can't get away with it.

20:44.940 --> 20:48.940
I expected something like this, and Evan Davis and the deputy are on their way up here now.

20:48.940 --> 20:52.940
You're going to have to do a lot of explaining about this accident, no matter how you make it look.

20:52.940 --> 20:53.940
Yeah?

20:53.940 --> 21:01.940
I suppose you're going to tell me that the United States Cavalry and the Texas Rangers are on their way up here to save you.

21:01.940 --> 21:03.940
I'm not bluffing, Mr. Miller.

21:03.940 --> 21:06.940
Neither am I, Mr. Braddock.

21:06.940 --> 21:08.940
Okay, Jeff.

21:08.940 --> 21:10.940
Take him into the mine.

21:18.940 --> 21:20.940
Gee, you don't need that much powder.

21:20.940 --> 21:22.940
Yes, I do.

21:22.940 --> 21:24.940
I want to cave him, a real cave in.

21:24.940 --> 21:27.940
So it'll take a couple of weeks to get him out of here.

21:27.940 --> 21:33.940
Come on, let's get out of here.

21:33.940 --> 21:34.940
Where's the key?

21:34.940 --> 21:35.940
I didn't take it.

21:35.940 --> 21:37.940
Well, I would have sworn I left it here.

21:37.940 --> 21:38.940
I always leave it here.

21:38.940 --> 21:39.940
It's on the floor.

21:39.940 --> 21:45.940
Get your hands up.

21:45.940 --> 21:49.940
If you're looking for the ignition key, I'm sure you've seen it somewhere.

21:49.940 --> 21:50.940
It's in the drawer.

21:50.940 --> 21:51.940
It's in the drawer.

21:51.940 --> 21:52.940
It's in the drawer.

21:52.940 --> 21:59.940
If you're looking for the ignition key, it's in my pocket.

21:59.940 --> 22:01.940
Where's Braddock?

22:01.940 --> 22:04.940
He's in the mine, collecting some more specimens.

22:04.940 --> 22:06.940
You seem to be in a hurry to get out of there.

22:06.940 --> 22:07.940
Sure we was.

22:07.940 --> 22:08.940
He's in there setting another fuse.

22:08.940 --> 22:11.940
He wants some more specimens for another assay.

22:11.940 --> 22:12.940
I think we'll go in and take a look.

22:12.940 --> 22:14.940
Better get the guns first.

22:19.940 --> 22:21.940
All right, get down and lead the way in.

22:21.940 --> 22:22.940
I ain't going in that tunnel.

22:22.940 --> 22:24.940
He's setting another fuse in there.

22:24.940 --> 22:27.940
And he's using more dynamite than makes sense to me.

22:27.940 --> 22:30.940
There's something wrong here.

22:30.940 --> 22:32.940
Go away there, you fool!

22:32.940 --> 22:47.940
Go!

22:47.940 --> 22:52.940
The syringe filled with gold chloride was injected into the bag like this.

22:52.940 --> 22:56.940
That's why your bull quartz assayed around $400 a ton.

22:56.940 --> 22:58.940
Miller managed to put too much salt in the food,

22:58.940 --> 23:01.940
and then he always arranged to have an empty canteen.

23:01.940 --> 23:04.940
Then he had Ritter hiding out in the bushes down by the spring.

23:04.940 --> 23:08.940
It only took him a few seconds to inject the gold chloride into the sack

23:08.940 --> 23:11.940
and then duck back into the bushes again.

23:11.940 --> 23:13.940
And when they assayed it and the chloride burned away,

23:13.940 --> 23:15.940
the gold was left in the specimens.

23:15.940 --> 23:17.940
Simple, but effective.

23:17.940 --> 23:20.940
Until I went back over every step three or four times,

23:20.940 --> 23:24.940
I forgot about that important few seconds the bag was out of my sight.

23:24.940 --> 23:28.940
The clue that put me back on the right track was the salty food.

23:28.940 --> 23:31.940
Well, these two won't be salting any more mines for a long time,

23:31.940 --> 23:35.940
especially with an attempted murder after face on top of grand larceny.

23:35.940 --> 23:38.940
Let's get started.

23:38.940 --> 23:41.940
I'm a pretty good judge of character. A policeman has to be.

23:41.940 --> 23:46.940
But old Dad Miller's innocent face and kind blue eyes would have fooled anyone.

23:46.940 --> 23:48.940
So always remember that in a confidence game,

23:48.940 --> 23:51.940
the crook rarely ever looks like the crook.

23:51.940 --> 23:53.940
If he did, he wouldn't be able to get your confidence.

23:53.940 --> 23:57.940
So always beware of anyone who approaches you with a story of easy money.

23:57.940 --> 23:59.940
Something for nothing.

23:59.940 --> 24:28.940
Remember, it could happen to you.

